---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Support"
  description="Lightning tipping and Nostr comments on Proof of Worth: how both work, and where the deliberate limits are."
>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow"><Icon name="bolt" size={14} /> Value4Value</span>
      <h1>Lightning &amp; Nostr</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Good product analysis takes research time. Every product page has a Zap button
        that lets you support the author directly via Lightning, and a comment section
        over the decentralized Nostr protocol – open to everyone, even without a wallet
        or a Nostr extension, both without any detour through the project.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid--2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3><Icon name="bolt" size={20} /> Lightning tipping — live</h3>
          <p>
            On every product page you can choose an amount and zap it via Lightning.
            Technically this runs over LNURL-Pay (LUD-16) directly to the author's
            Lightning address: one click via WebLN if you have a wallet extension
            installed (e.g. Alby), or a QR code / copyable invoice for a mobile wallet
            otherwise.
          </p>
          <p class="status status--live">No backend of our own, no money on our side:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The website never holds keys or funds</li>
            <li>A zap goes 1:1 from your wallet to the author's wallet</li>
            <li>Authors add their own Lightning address in <code>src/data/contributors.ts</code></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3><Icon name="nodes" size={20} /> Nostr comments &amp; zaps — live</h3>
          <p>
            Every product page has a comment section over the decentralized Nostr
            protocol (NIP-22). With a Nostr extension such as
            <a href="https://getalby.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alby</a>
            or <a href="https://github.com/fiatjaf/nos2x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nos2x</a>
            you can sign directly from the browser – no account with us needed.
          </p>
          <p class="status status--live">Comments can be zapped too:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Signed zap requests plus zap receipts publicly visible on relays (NIP-57)</li>
            <li>The zap goes to the Lightning address stored in the commenter's Nostr profile</li>
            <li>Without an address in that profile, the zap button on that comment is disabled</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card next-step">
        <h3><Icon name="message" size={18} /> Commenting without any wallet or extension — live</h3>
        <p>
          You don't need a Nostr extension or a Lightning wallet to join the conversation:
          without an extension, your browser automatically creates an anonymous identity
          stored only there, and signs your comment with it – it still lands as a perfectly
          normal Nostr comment in the same thread.
        </p>
        <p class="status status--live">Two ways to clear the built-in spam protection:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Proof-of-work (default):</strong> your browser computes for a few seconds (NIP-13, runs in a Web Worker so the page doesn't freeze) – costs nothing but a bit of compute time, but makes mass spam expensive.</li>
          <li><strong>Lightning shortcut (optional):</strong> with a WebLN-capable wallet (e.g. Alby) you can skip the wait by sending 21 sats to the author – published instantly, plus a small bit of support for whoever wrote the piece.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card next-step">
        <h3><Icon name="scale" size={18} /> Known limits of this implementation</h3>
        <p>
          To be clear: a decentralized comment system involves trade-offs we're
          accepting deliberately rather than hiding.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>There's no moderation by us – comments land directly on public relays. Your Nostr client (Alby, Damus, Primal, …) brings its own mute/block lists.</li>
          <li>We query a fixed default set of relays (relay.damus.io, nos.lol, relay.nostr.band, relay.primal.net) – comments on other relays aren't necessarily visible here.</li>
          <li>The <code>lnurl</code> tag in the zap request carries the plain Lightning address rather than a strictly spec-compliant bech32-encoded LNURL – that's enough for payment and our own receipt counting, but may render slightly differently in more exotic Nostr clients.</li>
          <li>The anonymous browser identity (no extension) is deliberately weaker than a real Nostr extension: the secret key sits in your browser's <code>localStorage</code>, with no password protection. Clear your browser data or switch devices, and you lose access to that identity – already-published comments stay on the relays regardless. For anything that matters to you, we recommend a real extension.</li>
          <li>Proof-of-work is not an absolute defense, just a way to raise the cost of mass spam (the same principle as classic hashcash) – with enough computing power it could still be worked around.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card next-step">
        <h3>How to add your Lightning address</h3>
        <p>
          For the article zap: add yourself to <code>src/data/contributors.ts</code>
          with your author name (matching the <code>autor</code> field in your product
          files) and your Lightning address. Details and an example:
          <a href="/en/mitmachen#lightning">Contribute page</a>. For zaps on your
          <em>comments</em>, a <code>lud16</code> field in your regular Nostr profile
          (kind&nbsp;0) is enough – you manage that directly in your Nostr client, not
          in this repository.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .status { font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .status--todo { color: var(--color-warn); }
  .status--live { color: var(--color-moss-dark); }
  .card ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; color: var(--color-ink-soft); font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .card li { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .card code { background: var(--color-paper-dim); padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.85em; }
  .next-step { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .next-step h3 { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .next-step h3 :global(svg) { color: var(--color-bronze); }
</style>
